Kinora @PACKAGE_VERSION@
==========
@BUILD_NOTICE@A film player for AmigaOS 3.2 on a PiStorm with a Raspberry Pi 4 or CM4
under Emu68, in the style of Media Player Classic: one window, the picture
above simple playback controls, everything else in the menus. The Pi's
VideoCore decodes H.264 and H.265, the 68k the older codecs; the sound plays
through AHI, and text subtitles are drawn into the picture. Films play in the
window or full screen.


INSTALLING
----------

Double-click Install. It asks what to install and where the drawer "Kinora"
shall be made (the default is SYS:Utilities), and says which of the parts
Kinora needs (below) it did not find. Kinora can be installed first and those
parts later; until then films do not open.

  Kinora, its icon, Fonts/      the drawer chosen
  demuxer.library               LIBS:
  audiocodec.library            LIBS:
  ffmpegaudio.codec             LIBS:audiocodec/
  the video libraries:
    videocodec.library          LIBS:
    videocore.codec             LIBS:videocodec/ (H.264, pictures)
    rpivid.codec                LIBS:videocodec/ (H.265)
    ffmpegvideo.codec           LIBS:videocodec/ (the older codecs)
    VCInfo                      C:
  vcjpeg.datatype (if wanted)   SYS:Classes/DataTypes/, SYS:Storage/DataTypes/
  VCGrab (if wanted)            SYS:Tools/Commodities/
  FilmInfo (if wanted)          C:

The video libraries can also be installed alone, for other programs that use
them. Files already there are updated; the script asks before it replaces a
newer one with an older one.

To remove Kinora, delete its drawer, the files above and the AddDataTypes line
in S:User-Startup. Its settings are in ENVARC:Kinora.prefs.


WHAT IT NEEDS
-------------

  * AmigaOS 3.2 on a PiStorm with a Raspberry Pi 4 or CM4 under Emu68.
    A Pi 3 has no H.265 decoder and is untested.
  * vchiq.device, the link to the Pi's VideoCore, from the Emu68 driver
    stack (a part of its Install script):
    https://github.com/rondoval/emu68-driver-stack
  * Picasso96 with VideoCore.card.
  * AHI.

For full screen, give the graphics card 32 MB with this line in config.txt
on the Pi's boot partition, then restart the Pi (Ctrl-Amiga-Amiga, or off
and on):

  dtoverlay=videocore,mem=32

Full screen keeps four pictures in the card's memory. Emu68's default of
16 MB has room for one at 1080p, and full screen then plays less smoothly than
the window.


WHAT IT PLAYS
-------------

  Containers   Matroska and WebM (.mkv, .webm); MP4 and QuickTime (.mp4,
               .m4v, .mov); MPEG transport streams (.ts, .m2ts); MPEG
               programme streams, as VCDs and DVDs hold them (.mpg, .mpeg,
               .vob); AVI (.avi, .divx); MPEG-1 and MPEG-2 video alone
               (.m1v, .m2v)
  Video        H.264; H.265 (HEVC) on a Pi 4 or CM4; on the 68k: MPEG-1,
               MPEG-2, MPEG-4 part 2 (Xvid, DivX), DivX 3, H.263, Theora
  Audio        AAC (LC, HE, HE v2, also as LATM in transport streams); AC-3;
               E-AC-3; DTS; Dolby TrueHD; MP3; MP2; Opus; Vorbis; FLAC; PCM
               (8, 16, 24 and 32 bit, 32-bit float); a DVD's LPCM; IMA and
               Microsoft ADPCM; A-law and mu-law
  Subtitles    SubRip, ASS/SSA and MP4 timed text inside the film; a SubRip
               file (.srt) beside it or opened by hand

Not played: other video codecs (VP8, VP9, AV1, VC-1), picture subtitles
(Blu-ray PGS, DVD VobSub, DVB), a DVD's menus (its .vob files play one at a
time), and anything over the network. H.264 with B-pictures in AVI, which is
rare, comes out of order.

The codecs the 68k decodes take much of the processor: films of DVD size keep
up, larger ones may not. Then Kinora skips pictures and the sound goes on.
Interlaced video (from television and DVDs) is shown combed.

Sound with more than two channels is mixed down to stereo unless the AHI
unit's mode is a 7.1 one. Files over 2 GiB play from file systems that can
seek that far; PFS3 cannot.


USING IT
--------

Start Kinora from its icon, or from a shell with films to play:

  Kinora film.mkv other.mp4

Drop films on the window or on the icon, or open them from the Project menu.
Project > Close empties the player and keeps the playlist.

  Space, or a click on the picture     play, pause
  A double click on the picture        full screen and back
  Cursor left, right                   ten seconds back, on
  F                                    full screen and back; Esc leaves it
  A, S                                 the next audio track, subtitle track
  PgUp, PgDn                           the playlist's film before, the next
  J (with the right Amiga key)         jump to a time
  I                                    statistics in the picture's corner

In full screen the controls come up along the screen's foot when the pointer
moves. Moving in a film lands on the key frame before the place asked for,
which can be some seconds early.

Subtitles come from the film or from a .srt file of the same name beside it.
They are drawn with DejaVu Sans, which has Latin, Greek and Cyrillic letters;
for other scripts choose a TrueType or OpenType font that has them (Subtitles
menu, Font...). Scripts that join their letters or run from the right do not
come out right.

The Audio menu chooses the AHI unit. Kinora plays 7.1 where the unit's mode in
AHI's prefs is a 7.1 one, and stereo otherwise. While a film is loaded Kinora
holds the unit's sound hardware, so other programs may not be heard through
it. With "Share the unit with other programs" (Audio menu) they can, and
Kinora plays 6 dB quieter; it shares the unit that way too when another
program already holds it.

The unit, sharing it, the volume, the font and the last drawer are
remembered.


WHEN PICTURES ARE DROPPED, OR A FILM DOES NOT PLAY
--------------------------------------------------

The key I shows what is playing, which codec drivers decode it, and whether
it keeps up. The ARexx command STATISTICS answers the same and more, for a
report of a film that does not play as it should.

If you use Executive, it moves programs' priorities by how much they use the
processor, and in full screen that costs Kinora pictures. To keep Kinora's
own priorities, add this line to S:Executive.prefs, with tabs between its
fields, and reboot:

  TASK		Kinora*		NOSCHEDULE

ExecutivePrefs makes the same entry: on its Tasks page a new one of type TASK,
named Kinora*, its action NOSCHEDULE and its priority left empty.

VCInfo checks the video libraries and says what to fix:

  VCInfo                the setup, each part OK or what to do
  VCInfo CODECS         what the codec drivers decode, encode and convert

When the VideoCore does not answer, it sends you to the driver stack's
VCHIQInfo.

FilmInfo tells what the libraries make of a film:

  FilmInfo                 the sound and video codecs that can be decoded
  FilmInfo film.mkv        the film's container, streams and chapters
  FilmInfo film.mkv AUDIO  decodes the sound and says what came out
  FilmInfo film.mkv VIDEO  decodes the video as fast as it can and says
                           what came out and how long it took


DRIVING IT FROM OTHER PROGRAMS
------------------------------

Kinora's ARexx port is KINORA. Its commands: PLAY, PAUSE, STOP, SEEK <ms>,
OPEN <file>, CLOSE, ADD <file>, NEXT, PREVIOUS, FULLSCREEN, SIZE <width>
<height>, FONT <file>, SUBTITLES <file>, STATUS, STATISTICS, TRACKS, AUDIO,
SUBTITLE, UNIT, VOLUME, ASPECT, DELAY, CHAPTERS, PLAYLIST and QUIT. Answers
come in RESULT.


